1
00:00:01,921 --> 00:00:03,442
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the ownership of your sense of self.

2
00:00:04,202 --> 00:00:05,042
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your mistake.

3
00:00:05,902 --> 00:00:07,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the existence of your sense of self.

4
00:00:08,843 --> 00:00:10,624
[SPEAKER_00]: But usually, people will jump into conclusion.

5
00:00:11,224 --> 00:00:12,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, no self means there is no self.

6
00:00:12,985 --> 00:00:15,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, who is then talking and deciding and making all these choices?

7
00:00:16,746 --> 00:00:19,787
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you actually started reading the suttas carefully, they would have never said there is no self.

8
00:00:19,807 --> 00:00:20,967
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, people are asking.

9
00:00:21,548 --> 00:00:23,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is Master Gautama saying there is self?

10
00:00:24,389 --> 00:00:24,989
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll be quiet.

11
00:00:25,609 --> 00:00:27,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, so Master Gautama is saying there is no self.

12
00:00:27,870 --> 00:00:28,630
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll be quiet.

13
00:00:29,835 --> 00:00:35,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Because either way, for this person, self is already there, ownership is already there, and he can't see the ownership.

14
00:00:36,177 --> 00:00:37,157
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the problem is.

15
00:00:38,318 --> 00:00:40,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you feel like you're your own?

16
00:00:42,139 --> 00:00:42,299
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

17
00:00:42,959 --> 00:00:43,379
[SPEAKER_00]: How come?

18
00:00:43,959 --> 00:00:48,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I cannot control my... Well, in hindsight, when you think about it, sure.

19
00:00:48,601 --> 00:00:51,642
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... say... say something that, eh...

20
00:00:53,086 --> 00:00:54,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that you wish for comes your way.

21
00:00:54,767 --> 00:00:56,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you happy and elated on account of it?

22
00:00:57,429 --> 00:01:00,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that you don't wish for comes your way.

23
00:01:00,051 --> 00:01:00,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you sad about it?

24
00:01:00,971 --> 00:01:05,454
[SPEAKER_00]: It means there is ownership in regard to the senses that can experience these things.

25
00:01:05,894 --> 00:01:08,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So then you can't say, oh, I don't regard myself as my own.

26
00:01:08,376 --> 00:01:17,562
[SPEAKER_00]: You might wish to not regard yourself as your own, but practically, facts are showing you that you do, because you still are liable to suffer and feel on account of change of things.

27
00:01:18,492 --> 00:01:19,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's really the problem.

28
00:01:19,493 --> 00:01:21,355
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all about denying things, denying yourself.

29
00:01:21,816 --> 00:01:27,042
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just gratuitous ownership that we exercise over our own senses.

30
00:01:28,043 --> 00:01:31,027
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you wonder whether you're exercising ownership or not, just ask yourself.

31
00:01:31,087 --> 00:01:33,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I suffer if something changes that I don't like?

32
00:01:34,411 --> 00:01:37,835
[SPEAKER_00]: If you wouldn't suffer, means, okay, I'm along the right path.

33
00:01:38,756 --> 00:01:41,417
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, that self-honesty needs to become first.

34
00:01:41,497 --> 00:01:49,441
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha said, a person needs to understand there is good action, there is bad action, there is a good result, bad result, there is the given, there is this, there is the practice.

35
00:01:50,101 --> 00:01:56,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if if a person is denying all of that, then exactly, they wouldn't necessarily see where the problem is and where to apply their effort.

36
00:01:56,964 --> 00:02:00,366
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it would be like, oh, there is no self, so what is there for me to do anyway?

37
00:02:01,326 --> 00:02:02,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there is no self.

38
00:02:02,286 --> 00:02:02,887
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say that.

39
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:03,667
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is suffering.

40
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:05,487
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still liable to suffering.

41
00:02:05,547 --> 00:02:06,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you need to do.

42
00:02:06,908 --> 00:02:10,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And once you start doing that rightly, you get to understand how that's what it would have meant by not-self.

43
00:02:10,449 --> 00:02:11,389
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what the suffering is.

44
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:17,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Not any kind of random theoretical scientific sense of experiment that proves that nothing's real and whatnot.

45
00:02:18,291 --> 00:02:23,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when the... when he approached the first disciples after his enlightenment,

46
00:02:25,182 --> 00:02:26,944
[SPEAKER_00]: They ask him, oh, what are you teaching?

47
00:02:27,024 --> 00:02:29,345
[SPEAKER_00]: And he didn't say, oh, I'm telling you there is no self.

48
00:02:29,365 --> 00:02:31,907
[SPEAKER_00]: He just said, there is suffering, and I'm teaching freedom from it.

49
00:02:32,528 --> 00:02:36,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's something that everybody can universally relate to.

50
00:02:37,931 --> 00:02:44,676
[SPEAKER_00]: When he met the man before that, in the same sutta, when he was going down the road, and this man sees him and says, oh, you look very bright, very composed.

51
00:02:46,057 --> 00:02:48,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Whose teaching did you follow to, like, develop those faculties?

52
00:02:48,859 --> 00:02:51,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, I follow no one's teaching.

53
00:02:51,201 --> 00:02:52,021
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm my own leader.

54
00:02:52,061 --> 00:02:53,142
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm the supreme ruler of

55
00:02:53,549 --> 00:02:54,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Humans and gods, I'm the best.

56
00:02:55,751 --> 00:02:56,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is pure fact.

57
00:02:56,832 --> 00:02:57,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing conceited about it.

58
00:02:58,653 --> 00:03:03,838
[SPEAKER_00]: But in this man's eyes, through his own conceit, all he could see is like, I am the best, and so be it, and left it.

59
00:03:04,498 --> 00:03:06,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't want to hear the rest, what the Buddha had to say.

60
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:13,525
[SPEAKER_00]: So clearly, by the time the Buddha reached these other disciples, he realized, if he approaches them with a sense of, I know what the truth is, they're going to be on defensive.

61
00:03:14,266 --> 00:03:16,528
[SPEAKER_00]: But he does know what the truth is, but people won't hear it.

62
00:03:17,154 --> 00:03:22,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, he related that which they can relate to internally, which is, you're liable to suffering.

63
00:03:22,696 --> 00:03:25,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't necessarily suffer, you are liable.

64
00:03:25,718 --> 00:03:26,838
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eventually, you will have to.

65
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:28,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Old age, sickness, death.

66
00:03:28,199 --> 00:03:28,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Inevitable.

67
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:30,900
[SPEAKER_00]: And, I'm teaching freedom from it.

68
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:31,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay.

69
00:03:32,341 --> 00:03:33,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, yeah, I want that.

70
00:03:34,121 --> 00:03:35,462
[SPEAKER_00]: And, that's how they start to listen to him.

71
00:03:35,482 --> 00:03:37,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, they try to dismiss him first, because...

72
00:03:44,225 --> 00:03:46,165
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's in answer to the question, there is no self.

73
00:03:46,185 --> 00:03:48,566
[SPEAKER_00]: There is self, but you should try and not own it.

74
00:03:48,766 --> 00:03:51,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can just say, oh, there is no self, there is self, there is both, there is neither.

75
00:03:52,507 --> 00:03:55,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Factually, you remain liable to suffering and you don't know the way out.

76
00:03:56,308 --> 00:03:58,509
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the core of the teaching, suffering and freedom from it.

77
00:03:59,889 --> 00:04:01,949
[SPEAKER_00]: And that brings me to practice of metta.

78
00:04:02,009 --> 00:04:04,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you want to ask yourself, why do you want to do it?

79
00:04:04,350 --> 00:04:04,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you doing?

80
00:04:04,930 --> 00:04:08,911
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the purpose of your meditation?

81
00:04:09,891 --> 00:04:12,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Personally, I want to, like,

82
00:04:13,787 --> 00:04:14,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Focus myself.

83
00:04:16,009 --> 00:04:17,210
[SPEAKER_02]: That is my main thing.

84
00:04:17,510 --> 00:04:19,271
[SPEAKER_00]: And what would be the purpose of that?

85
00:04:20,212 --> 00:04:21,153
[SPEAKER_02]: To calm my mind.

86
00:04:22,214 --> 00:04:26,997
[SPEAKER_00]: And what would be the purpose of that?

87
00:04:27,037 --> 00:04:28,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you prefer a calm mind?

88
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:32,241
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, yes, free of suffering, then.

89
00:04:32,962 --> 00:04:35,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Because a non-calm mind bothers you.

90
00:04:35,644 --> 00:04:40,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why go a round way about it, when you can start thinking about what makes calm mind calm?

91
00:04:42,103 --> 00:04:43,724
[SPEAKER_00]: What disrupts the mind?

92
00:04:44,805 --> 00:04:46,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Does sense desire disrupt the mind?

93
00:04:47,386 --> 00:04:49,047
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want something that you don't have?

94
00:04:50,388 --> 00:04:54,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Does, you know, somebody says something upsetting and you refuse to let go of it.

95
00:04:54,990 --> 00:04:56,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that disrupt your mind?

96
00:04:56,971 --> 00:04:59,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize you don't need to do meditation to calm your mind.

97
00:04:59,773 --> 00:05:02,714
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to stop disrupting yourself with your own choices and actions.

98
00:05:03,715 --> 00:05:05,116
[SPEAKER_00]: And then meditation comes out of that.

99
00:05:06,356 --> 00:05:09,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually, people hear about metta meditation and

100
00:05:09,943 --> 00:05:16,567
[SPEAKER_00]: They use it wrongly in a sense of you try to replace issues with matter.

101
00:05:18,308 --> 00:05:20,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, if I do matter, it will come in place of this.

102
00:05:20,809 --> 00:05:23,511
[SPEAKER_00]: But the place of your problem is not in things.

103
00:05:24,071 --> 00:05:28,033
[SPEAKER_00]: The place of your disturbed mind is not in because somebody said something upsetting to you.

104
00:05:28,253 --> 00:05:31,775
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because your mind got upset on account of something that's being said to it.

105
00:05:33,416 --> 00:05:34,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't replace that.

106
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:37,901
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only train it to not do that anymore.

107
00:05:38,181 --> 00:05:40,582
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, I don't need to suffer amid suffering.

108
00:05:41,062 --> 00:05:47,404
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said, if the mind is developed, you would endure the unpleasant, harsh insults, words, attacks.

109
00:05:47,804 --> 00:05:51,205
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you endure wind, cold, sun, like elements.

110
00:05:52,066 --> 00:05:53,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely nothing personal about it.

111
00:05:54,566 --> 00:05:57,187
[SPEAKER_00]: But you take the ownership of it, and it's automatically personal.

112
00:05:57,668 --> 00:06:01,549
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you don't have to deal with it, and then you hear about Metta, and not you specifically, everybody does that.

113
00:06:02,192 --> 00:06:08,695
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you hope, if I develop this meditation, some meditation will push this whole thing out, and I will never be able to get angry or something like that.

114
00:06:09,396 --> 00:06:11,637
[SPEAKER_00]: You get angry by giving in to anger.

115
00:06:12,457 --> 00:06:17,760
[SPEAKER_00]: You get lustful by giving in to lust, by making choices based upon it, by having non-restraint.

116
00:06:19,221 --> 00:06:28,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what's in common, by the way, to pursuing sensual desire, acting out of anger, acting out of carelessness, is lack of restraint, lack of self-control.

117
00:06:29,626 --> 00:06:29,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So then,

118
00:06:30,710 --> 00:06:36,392
[SPEAKER_00]: You could draw a line and say that, oh, so your mind gets disturbed when you don't have enough control over yourself.

119
00:06:37,152 --> 00:06:38,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's entirely up to you.

120
00:06:38,473 --> 00:06:39,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody can control you for you.

121
00:06:41,634 --> 00:06:45,415
[SPEAKER_00]: And people don't have enough control over themselves because they don't think enough about it.

122
00:06:46,643 --> 00:07:01,315
[SPEAKER_00]: They expect circumstances to control them, whatever tradition, duty, values, and they do to some extent, but you want to take it on a new level, whereby you take control of your sense restraint, of your verbal actions, of your physical actions.

123
00:07:02,135 --> 00:07:03,517
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already the beginning of wisdom.

124
00:07:03,997 --> 00:07:06,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Five precepts, or then take it further, eight precepts, and so on.

125
00:07:08,220 --> 00:07:10,842
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we have to always start with the five precepts.

126
00:07:11,363 --> 00:07:13,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just you have to start, you always stay within them, by the way.

127
00:07:14,501 --> 00:07:18,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even when you start practicing meditation, then, you don't do it in... it's within the five precepts.

128
00:07:18,383 --> 00:07:20,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just continuation of the same principles.

129
00:07:21,765 --> 00:07:25,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Of that sense of non-disturbed mind that comes from controlling your actions.

130
00:07:25,348 --> 00:07:30,411
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, literally, in its core, the Buddha said he teaches action and freedom from action.

131
00:07:31,571 --> 00:07:32,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And action is already a choice.

132
00:07:33,312 --> 00:07:34,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already on a level of an action.

133
00:07:35,614 --> 00:07:36,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey boy, it's okay.

134
00:07:37,035 --> 00:07:37,555
[SPEAKER_00]: It's okay, man.

135
00:07:39,617 --> 00:07:42,520
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I think he's just strangers in his space.

136
00:07:43,501 --> 00:07:49,026
[SPEAKER_04]: He wants to let you know who the boss is.

137
00:07:52,750 --> 00:08:00,938
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I mean, like, you have to be very disciplined and following precepts and all that before we can actually develop wisdom.

138
00:08:01,198 --> 00:08:02,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's already the beginning of wisdom.

139
00:08:04,815 --> 00:08:06,097
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a mistake to separate it.

140
00:08:06,358 --> 00:08:09,101
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a mistake to think, oh, wisdom will then come after I do this.

141
00:08:09,241 --> 00:08:11,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing that is already the beginning of wisdom.

142
00:08:11,645 --> 00:08:20,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're already becoming transparent in regard to your own motives, where your intentions and choices are rooted, and then you would know, oh, this is unwholesome.

143
00:08:21,235 --> 00:08:24,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, this is rooted in completely unskillful desires in me.

144
00:08:24,977 --> 00:08:26,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Every... the world can praise it.

145
00:08:26,557 --> 00:08:27,798
[SPEAKER_00]: They can say, this is a great thing to do.

146
00:08:27,818 --> 00:08:28,718
[SPEAKER_00]: You have so much passion.

147
00:08:29,338 --> 00:08:33,980
[SPEAKER_00]: But from the standpoint of wanting to have a mind that is calm, you realize, no, this is incompatible.

148
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:35,180
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't have that.

149
00:08:36,381 --> 00:08:40,862
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why people don't entertain these things is because they don't... they don't accept, they don't look at it.

150
00:08:40,962 --> 00:08:41,703
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not transparent.

151
00:08:41,723 --> 00:08:42,283
[SPEAKER_00]: They ignore it.

152
00:08:43,517 --> 00:08:45,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everybody wants to have their mind calm.

153
00:08:45,759 --> 00:08:46,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if people say they don't, they do.

154
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:50,041
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you always pursue pleasure and stuff, because it's...

155
00:08:50,081 --> 00:08:52,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Satisfaction, it's already a form of calmness.

156
00:08:53,524 --> 00:09:02,950
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why people are dependent on it, because they don't know any other type of satisfaction, except, like, sensual satisfaction, or satisfaction of real will, and retaliation, and so on.

157
00:09:05,992 --> 00:09:07,633
[SPEAKER_04]: First, I need to ask a question.

158
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:13,080
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

159
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:15,861
[SPEAKER_03]: So, my question is...

160
00:09:16,462 --> 00:09:36,509
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if the Buddha intended to not be worshipped as a god or any part of his physical self, idolized, as a Buddhist, is it acceptable to visit places like the Dalada, Maligawa, to worship his sacred tooth or Adam's peak to worship his footprint?

161
00:09:37,029 --> 00:09:38,550
[SPEAKER_00]: The... the thing is...

162
00:09:40,304 --> 00:09:46,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Not people, not all people do the... neither Buddhists nor do they practice for the same reason.

163
00:09:47,328 --> 00:09:55,393
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you visit Maligawa, because it will just remind you of the Buddha, and the possibility of enlightenment, that's fine.

164
00:09:56,094 --> 00:09:59,176
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha did say, yeah, when they asked him, what should we worship when you die?

165
00:10:00,517 --> 00:10:02,898
[SPEAKER_00]: There were no statues, and he said, make no statues of me.

166
00:10:03,058 --> 00:10:05,400
[SPEAKER_00]: He was only came later with the Greeks, when they came to India.

167
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:08,642
[SPEAKER_00]: But it got quickly established, because it's easier for people to,

168
00:10:13,339 --> 00:10:16,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Rather than these symbols that already require a degree of wisdom and recognition.

169
00:10:17,165 --> 00:10:20,190
[SPEAKER_00]: However, the Buddha did say you can worship the symbol like the Bodhi tree.

170
00:10:20,791 --> 00:10:22,113
[SPEAKER_00]: They will remind you of enlightenment.

171
00:10:22,153 --> 00:10:23,395
[SPEAKER_00]: You might think about enlightenment.

172
00:10:23,415 --> 00:10:26,340
[SPEAKER_00]: You might spur something in you that you might maybe want to do it.

173
00:10:27,062 --> 00:10:33,549
[SPEAKER_00]: You should worship, like, the sign of an alms bowl, which means there's a possibility to give up the world and be a renunciate.

174
00:10:34,390 --> 00:10:36,933
[SPEAKER_00]: An empty seat that reminds you of the teaching and so on.

175
00:10:36,993 --> 00:10:41,097
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all these symbolic things, just it might make somebody think about the dhamma.

176
00:10:41,197 --> 00:10:41,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

177
00:10:41,658 --> 00:10:43,079
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the sole purpose of worship.

178
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:44,982
[SPEAKER_00]: But nowadays, most...

179
00:10:45,928 --> 00:10:47,369
[SPEAKER_00]: People worship out of superstition.

180
00:10:48,209 --> 00:10:51,130
[SPEAKER_00]: And that has absolutely nothing to do with Buddha's teaching or Buddhism.

181
00:10:51,670 --> 00:10:53,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But that is what Buddhism is today.

182
00:10:53,491 --> 00:10:54,831
[SPEAKER_00]: What every other religion is today.

183
00:10:54,871 --> 00:10:56,172
[SPEAKER_00]: It's firstly superstition.

184
00:10:56,992 --> 00:11:01,013
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, within that, those who are interested in more than just superstition get to find out the teaching.

185
00:11:02,394 --> 00:11:08,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you visit Maligawa so you can pass your test exams, it has absolutely nothing to do with anything the Buddha taught.

186
00:11:10,336 --> 00:11:14,878
[SPEAKER_00]: If you visit Maligawa because, you know, you want your wishes to come true, again, it's completely misplaced.

187
00:11:16,838 --> 00:11:26,766
[SPEAKER_00]: If you visit, because... and oh, another way of misplacing it, if you think you can visit shrines and somehow get closer to enlightenment simply by physically visiting, you know, stupas and whatnot, there's absolutely nothing to do with it.

188
00:11:28,308 --> 00:11:31,851
[SPEAKER_00]: The enlightenment can only come, like, through the self-restraint, understanding and wisdom.

189
00:11:32,171 --> 00:11:34,633
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, in that sense, this can be helpful.

190
00:11:35,554 --> 00:11:40,799
[SPEAKER_00]: You get inspired, maybe, and then you want to read the Buddha's teaching, once you visited some statue or something.

191
00:11:41,559 --> 00:11:43,361
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a very remote sense, not, like,

192
00:11:43,863 --> 00:11:48,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And I often come to people who think if as long as you worship, you're somehow getting closer to nibbāna.

193
00:11:49,265 --> 00:11:50,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's just wishful thinking.

194
00:11:51,626 --> 00:11:53,327
[SPEAKER_02]: That is how it is presented in life.

195
00:11:53,367 --> 00:11:57,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, because it's much easier than if I tell you, no, you need to be perfectly restrained.

196
00:11:57,409 --> 00:11:58,609
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to give up things you like.

197
00:11:59,209 --> 00:12:00,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes endure things you don't like.

198
00:12:00,770 --> 00:12:01,550
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to hear that.

199
00:12:01,830 --> 00:12:03,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Only few people can hear that and accept it.

200
00:12:04,391 --> 00:12:08,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's easier for me as well to just, yeah, just pay respect to chandas and, you know.

201
00:12:08,842 --> 00:12:09,502
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be fine.

202
00:12:09,742 --> 00:12:18,725
[SPEAKER_01]: What did Buddha say about āmīsa pūjā and pratipātti, you know, those days, his teachings?

203
00:12:19,085 --> 00:12:19,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

204
00:12:20,306 --> 00:12:22,927
[SPEAKER_00]: He said about, like, alms and being generous.

205
00:12:23,447 --> 00:12:25,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just to monks, but in general.

206
00:12:25,587 --> 00:12:26,368
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like making money.

207
00:12:26,388 --> 00:12:28,188
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not bringing you closer to enlightenment.

208
00:12:28,768 --> 00:12:34,090
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just bringing you closer to, like, a more wholesome mind, better rebirths, and so on.

209
00:12:35,230 --> 00:12:37,071
[SPEAKER_00]: But enlightenment, you need to abandon it all.

210
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:41,181
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't, like, give your way in terms of gifts to enlightenment.

211
00:12:41,301 --> 00:12:42,241
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't work like that.

212
00:12:45,182 --> 00:12:46,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... see, that's the problem.

213
00:12:46,123 --> 00:12:48,983
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a problem that you don't necessarily pursue enlightenment.

214
00:12:49,624 --> 00:13:00,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is when people then intentionally develop a view that, oh, I don't need to pursue it, and I can do this, and by doing these random things, I'm somehow also getting closer to it.

215
00:13:00,987 --> 00:13:03,848
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you never really admit to yourself that you're not.

216
00:13:04,357 --> 00:13:07,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting closer to enlightenment just by random acts of generosity.

217
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:11,601
[SPEAKER_02]: And even good karma, when you accumulate so many...

218
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:12,001
[SPEAKER_02]: It's still karma.

219
00:13:12,021 --> 00:13:14,102
[SPEAKER_02]: It's still karma and you still have to... That's the whole point.

220
00:13:14,122 --> 00:13:14,782
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

221
00:13:15,103 --> 00:13:17,084
[SPEAKER_00]: He teaches action and freedom from action.

222
00:13:18,204 --> 00:13:20,045
[SPEAKER_00]: And action is good action and bad action.

223
00:13:20,245 --> 00:13:26,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you need to replace bad action with good action, but now you need to abandon that as well, if you really want enlightenment.

224
00:13:27,349 --> 00:13:32,852
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that, you can't have, like, good action somehow result in self-canceling action and you become fully enlightened arahant.

225
00:13:34,891 --> 00:13:36,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, literally, we would just prescribe.

226
00:13:36,372 --> 00:13:38,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Give this, give this, give this, and you'll be enlightened.

227
00:13:38,373 --> 00:13:39,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But we didn't.

228
00:13:39,874 --> 00:13:40,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Never.

229
00:13:42,155 --> 00:13:43,855
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to come within anyways, right?

230
00:13:43,876 --> 00:13:51,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody cannot, like... Somebody can buy you, but you... That's why he himself said, whatever is to obtain, to be obtained by the manly effort, manly...

231
00:13:51,279 --> 00:13:51,759
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done it.

232
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:53,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it cannot be taken away.

233
00:13:53,720 --> 00:13:54,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you do it for yourself.

234
00:13:56,562 --> 00:13:58,403
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't depend on the external factors.

235
00:13:59,043 --> 00:14:02,425
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not given to you, like, you know, sudden divine providence.

236
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:06,522
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, no, it doesn't come that way.

237
00:14:08,283 --> 00:14:16,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, that's what I mean, like, if you're clear as to why and what for, and keep clarifying if you're not clear, then you get to realize what you really want.

238
00:14:18,311 --> 00:14:21,073
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get to do it properly as well, without misconceiving it.

239
00:14:21,813 --> 00:14:26,237
[SPEAKER_00]: And you might be fine, like, oh, maybe, like, enlightenment seems too far out of my reach.

240
00:14:26,957 --> 00:14:35,283
[SPEAKER_00]: But let me then focus on things that are not without marriage, that the Buddha himself, whom I respect enough to follow the teaching, said a layman should do.

241
00:14:36,464 --> 00:14:42,167
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's when laymen, like, especially in the West, oh, I'm Buddhist for 20 years, practicing, and they don't even keep the precepts.

242
00:14:43,048 --> 00:14:47,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing meditation, retreats and stuff, still drink alcohol and stuff, like, no restraint.

243
00:14:48,812 --> 00:14:52,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And like, well, see, the contradiction you're living is because of your own ignorance.

244
00:14:52,114 --> 00:14:52,895
[SPEAKER_00]: No one else's fault.

245
00:14:53,703 --> 00:15:00,286
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to make the effort, instead of having a wishful thinking of your jhānas and whatnot, focus on what you are first, and perfect that.

246
00:15:00,806 --> 00:15:04,868
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll see whether you're happy with it, or whether you will become a monk, or commit your last rites to the retreat.

247
00:15:05,528 --> 00:15:08,249
[SPEAKER_00]: But they want to have it all at the same time.

248
00:15:08,329 --> 00:15:15,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can also practice for enlightenment, and I can also, you know, have my desires as a lay person, and so on.

249
00:15:16,693 --> 00:15:18,454
[SPEAKER_03]: So, essentially, you have to pick one.

250
00:15:19,295 --> 00:15:22,117
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to recognize what your situation is and take responsibility for it.

251
00:15:22,237 --> 00:15:22,598
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

252
00:15:22,878 --> 00:15:23,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

253
00:15:23,658 --> 00:15:24,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what I mean.

254
00:15:24,699 --> 00:15:27,441
[SPEAKER_00]: If you renounce the world, it's because you chose to do so.

255
00:15:27,521 --> 00:15:29,543
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't renounce the world, it's because you chose to do so.

256
00:15:30,804 --> 00:15:35,207
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no, like, external authority or criteria that demands from you to stay like a person or not.

257
00:15:36,568 --> 00:15:37,709
[SPEAKER_04]: It's purely by choice.

258
00:15:38,149 --> 00:15:38,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

259
00:15:38,590 --> 00:15:41,452
[SPEAKER_00]: But every choice means you're responsible for it.

260
00:15:41,652 --> 00:15:44,234
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the bit that people try to avoid because it's unpleasant.

261
00:15:45,122 --> 00:15:55,284
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if I accept responsibility that I'm not renouncing the world, means I have to accept responsibility for anything that comes at me from that world that I haven't renounced.

262
00:15:56,544 --> 00:16:00,445
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to accept responsibility for anything that results out of my unenlightened mind.

263
00:16:00,705 --> 00:16:05,546
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't complain about it, because I choose to remain in the environment where these things can happen.

264
00:16:07,726 --> 00:16:11,387
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not easy to bear necessarily for everyone, so people would then

265
00:16:12,327 --> 00:16:19,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to kind of mask that and then, at the same time, develop a sense that... but no, I am still practicing renunciation and everything, while I'm not, really.

266
00:16:20,709 --> 00:16:25,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just need to learn how to be really, really, even brutally honest with yourself, nobody else.

267
00:16:26,691 --> 00:16:28,771
[SPEAKER_00]: What are you doing and why are you doing it?

268
00:16:29,991 --> 00:16:30,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

269
00:16:30,232 --> 00:16:30,912
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to meditate.

270
00:16:30,972 --> 00:16:31,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

271
00:16:31,352 --> 00:16:31,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

272
00:16:31,692 --> 00:16:31,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

273
00:16:31,992 --> 00:16:32,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

274
00:16:32,252 --> 00:16:32,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

275
00:16:32,852 --> 00:16:33,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I don't want to suffer.

276
00:16:34,273 --> 00:16:35,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do I suffer?

277
00:16:35,053 --> 00:16:36,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I suffer when I don't meditate?

278
00:16:37,013 --> 00:16:37,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily.

279
00:16:37,713 --> 00:16:40,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I suffer when I act out unwholesomely?

280
00:16:41,179 --> 00:16:42,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's the root of my suffering.

281
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:46,282
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me deal with that and then maybe meditation will become more obvious later.

282
00:16:47,262 --> 00:16:52,585
[SPEAKER_00]: But for people, as I said, meditation becomes extension of what I want fulfilled for me.

283
00:16:52,645 --> 00:16:53,545
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it won't work.

284
00:16:55,686 --> 00:16:59,948
[SPEAKER_02]: Not in a bad manner, at least.

285
00:17:00,168 --> 00:17:04,650
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I have a question I was thinking about.

286
00:17:04,731 --> 00:17:07,372
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I was wondering what

287
00:17:08,872 --> 00:17:12,194
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you decide what is like a comfort or something that you need to give up?

288
00:17:12,354 --> 00:17:13,495
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I just... my question...

289
00:17:13,515 --> 00:17:17,937
[SPEAKER_03]: So, also another part I was thinking about was the use of technology in Buddhism.

290
00:17:18,789 --> 00:17:24,012
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, you know, like phones, for example, is that allowed?

291
00:17:24,072 --> 00:17:24,972
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that not?

292
00:17:25,012 --> 00:17:28,034
[SPEAKER_03]: If you were pursuing the path of enlightenment... Those are tools.

293
00:17:28,534 --> 00:17:29,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

294
00:17:30,095 --> 00:17:33,056
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... tools are not, like, necessarily wrong.

295
00:17:33,236 --> 00:17:34,377
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the purpose of the use.

296
00:17:35,057 --> 00:17:41,360
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you use your phone to cheat, steal, engage in sensuality, spread hate, it's wrong.

297
00:17:41,380 --> 00:17:43,021
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not the usage of the phone that's wrong.

298
00:17:43,061 --> 00:17:46,363
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your intention and your action that's being acted out of the phone.

299
00:17:47,340 --> 00:17:51,763
[SPEAKER_00]: If you use the phone to google the suttas and read the translations online, it's of course not wrong.

300
00:17:52,504 --> 00:17:56,286
[SPEAKER_00]: If you use the phone to communicate when communication is needed, it's not wrong.

301
00:17:56,326 --> 00:17:59,709
[SPEAKER_00]: If you use the phone to just chit-chat random gossip, it's wrong.

302
00:17:59,749 --> 00:18:03,712
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, not because of the phone, but because of the intention behind your action and what you're doing.

303
00:18:04,972 --> 00:18:05,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why it's not like...

304
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:07,482
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not prescribed.

305
00:18:07,622 --> 00:18:10,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, you have those Christian orders where you shun.

306
00:18:11,129 --> 00:18:12,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you must shun anything new and stuff.

307
00:18:13,173 --> 00:18:13,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at all.

308
00:18:13,894 --> 00:18:14,515
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the tool.

309
00:18:15,216 --> 00:18:17,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You mustn't shun your responsibility for your actions.

310
00:18:18,448 --> 00:18:23,831
[SPEAKER_00]: But people try to, like, like, like, throw away the tools, because then they would blame responsibility on the tools.

311
00:18:24,011 --> 00:18:24,931
[SPEAKER_00]: See, it's the phone's fault.

312
00:18:25,211 --> 00:18:27,192
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the Internet's fault that I can't be restrained.

313
00:18:27,292 --> 00:18:28,973
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's your own fault that you're not restrained.

314
00:18:29,633 --> 00:18:31,894
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so technology is okay in the lab, just...

315
00:18:31,994 --> 00:18:32,294
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

316
00:18:32,334 --> 00:18:33,635
[SPEAKER_03]: It's why you're using it for.

317
00:18:33,895 --> 00:18:34,696
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

318
00:18:34,736 --> 00:18:37,037
[SPEAKER_00]: It always defines whether it's right or wrong.

319
00:18:37,437 --> 00:18:37,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

320
00:18:38,273 --> 00:18:39,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it can't be a blanket one.

321
00:18:39,535 --> 00:18:44,801
[SPEAKER_00]: All technologies is absolutely fine, or it's absolutely wrong, because people will use it for different purposes.

322
00:18:44,841 --> 00:18:51,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, like knives, forks, you can use to harm somebody with it, or you can very practically, you can survive in the wilderness without it.

323
00:18:51,789 --> 00:18:52,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

324
00:19:00,250 --> 00:19:06,613
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, another question I had was, okay, so the concept of, you know, reincarnation and good karma and bad karma.

325
00:19:06,633 --> 00:19:21,038
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you know, I guess, like, if my understanding is correct, when you accumulate good karma and bad karma, it is, it goes, I guess, in terms of when you're being reborn, in terms of, you know, how you'll be reborn into your next life, essentially.

326
00:19:21,638 --> 00:19:25,180
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you, and, like, so my question is, I guess, essentially, like,

327
00:19:26,241 --> 00:19:27,742
[SPEAKER_03]: How about this current life?

328
00:19:27,763 --> 00:19:29,624
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, how do you end suffering in the present?

329
00:19:30,205 --> 00:19:30,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

330
00:19:30,445 --> 00:19:32,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, that's that's the point.

331
00:19:33,007 --> 00:19:38,773
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to uproot the desire from your mind and conceit, you would be uprooting any basis for suffering.

332
00:19:39,013 --> 00:19:39,333
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

333
00:19:39,814 --> 00:19:43,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you do it.

334
00:19:44,679 --> 00:19:47,761
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's something you can't do, in a sense of just perform.

335
00:19:47,781 --> 00:19:55,386
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, you can't just be generous, pay your way to enlightenment with just generosity or something, because that's just another level of doing.

336
00:19:56,166 --> 00:19:58,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the Buddha said, first, you need to be sense-restrained.

337
00:19:59,588 --> 00:20:07,453
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to keep the precepts and restrain your desires and only recognize things that, okay, I, you know, I need to give in to this or something like that, but not like...

338
00:20:08,557 --> 00:20:13,601
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a blanket mode of being whereby you always say yes and then only sometimes say no when you can't get what you want.

339
00:20:14,101 --> 00:20:14,941
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the other way around.

340
00:20:15,082 --> 00:20:20,966
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like, no, I'll keep myself in check only when I can't, then... but even then means I'm responsible for it.

341
00:20:20,986 --> 00:20:21,766
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first step.

342
00:20:22,447 --> 00:20:24,828
[SPEAKER_00]: There has to be, like, to fully accept that you are responsible.

343
00:20:25,269 --> 00:20:29,712
[SPEAKER_00]: See, people think they suffer because things are unpleasant.

344
00:20:29,732 --> 00:20:30,212
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't.

345
00:20:31,153 --> 00:20:34,295
[SPEAKER_00]: You suffer because you resist the displeasure that you feel.

346
00:20:35,166 --> 00:20:36,787
[SPEAKER_03]: So, suffering itself is a concept.

347
00:20:36,867 --> 00:20:37,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain is not suffering.

348
00:20:38,088 --> 00:20:38,328
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

349
00:20:39,188 --> 00:20:43,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Resisting pain, by default, is suffering.

350
00:20:43,431 --> 00:20:46,773
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the craving is the root of suffering, not feeling is the root of suffering.

351
00:20:47,253 --> 00:20:47,653
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

352
00:20:48,794 --> 00:20:56,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to be free from suffering in this life, you have to stop craving, stop resisting, stop indulging what you feel right here, right now.

353
00:20:58,760 --> 00:21:03,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And the first step is to stop acting blindly on account of what you feel right here, right now.

354
00:21:03,943 --> 00:21:04,203
[SPEAKER_04]: Right.

355
00:21:04,563 --> 00:21:13,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you get to round up and see, okay, so you're not acting by the body, you're not acting by the speech, and then you get to see how your mind is affected by what you feel right now.

356
00:21:13,208 --> 00:21:15,009
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the craving is, in your mind.

357
00:21:15,730 --> 00:21:23,634
[SPEAKER_00]: But you cannot prove the craving in your mind while your physical actions are still very much engaging and rooted in that same craving.

358
00:21:26,536 --> 00:21:28,477
[SPEAKER_03]: So, suffering itself is like how you...

359
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:39,787
[SPEAKER_03]: How you perceive something, or how you conceive, and how you conceive something, and that is what causes, brings you that suffering, right?

360
00:21:40,127 --> 00:21:40,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

361
00:21:41,147 --> 00:21:43,429
[SPEAKER_00]: It's slightly more immediate than that, though.

362
00:21:43,509 --> 00:21:49,212
[SPEAKER_00]: It's literally, when you are touched by disagreeable, your mind automatically leans away from it.

363
00:21:49,232 --> 00:21:49,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

364
00:21:49,973 --> 00:21:50,713
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the default.

365
00:21:51,334 --> 00:21:51,554
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

366
00:21:51,574 --> 00:21:55,336
[SPEAKER_00]: When you are touched by agreeable, your mind automatically leans towards it.

367
00:21:56,197 --> 00:21:58,198
[SPEAKER_00]: And is that leaning, is the root of suffering.

368
00:21:59,070 --> 00:22:01,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being touched in the first place.

369
00:22:01,812 --> 00:22:09,296
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, in the cases of fully enlightened monks, you know, sometimes they got attacked on the road and stabbed and stuff, and they didn't suffer on account of it.

370
00:22:10,397 --> 00:22:13,078
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean they didn't feel the physical discomfort on account of it.

371
00:22:14,099 --> 00:22:15,260
[SPEAKER_04]: They did.

372
00:22:15,920 --> 00:22:17,841
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize that was never really the problem.

373
00:22:17,861 --> 00:22:23,625
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was you not being able to not lean towards or against when you're touched by a feeling.

374
00:22:24,805 --> 00:22:26,826
[SPEAKER_00]: And you couldn't do it because you haven't understood it.

375
00:22:29,328 --> 00:22:38,392
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the sensory strain needs to come first, because by default, people would first and foremost blame the external world for whatever they felt.

376
00:22:38,412 --> 00:22:42,334
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I feel this suffering because such and such person upset me.

377
00:22:43,635 --> 00:22:54,420
[SPEAKER_00]: No, such and such person had something to say disagreeable to you, but you leaning away, trying to get rid of, trying to override the disagreeability in you, that's your suffering, not what they said.

378
00:22:55,633 --> 00:23:00,715
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you never look in that direction, you remain blaming them, and then feeling justified in hating them, and so on.

379
00:23:01,875 --> 00:23:06,957
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, that needs to be restrained, and then you get to understand where the problem really is.

380
00:23:07,057 --> 00:23:07,437
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

381
00:23:07,457 --> 00:23:07,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

