533
00:33:20,052 --> 00:33:27,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, you can continue acting like that, and that's why you remain liable to, when those things happen, remain liable to being affected by them.

534
00:33:27,598 --> 00:33:30,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can start uprooting those gratuitous wrong views.

535
00:33:31,521 --> 00:33:32,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but that's what we say.

536
00:33:32,842 --> 00:33:34,904
[SPEAKER_00]: You just say, oh, watch your nostrils and you'll be coming out.

537
00:33:34,924 --> 00:33:39,488
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he reflects on the nature of things, the nature of your actions, the nature of your choices.

538
00:33:40,329 --> 00:33:40,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about it.

539
00:33:41,650 --> 00:33:45,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by thinking about it, you stop ignoring the obvious, that you're not in control.

540
00:33:46,694 --> 00:33:50,957
[SPEAKER_00]: And people ignore the obvious, because lack of control, from the point of view of control, is very unpleasant.

541
00:33:52,298 --> 00:33:54,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's anxiety and fear and all.

542
00:33:55,281 --> 00:33:59,504
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's only so because you completely ignore it, and then only sometimes it's thrown on you.

543
00:33:59,564 --> 00:34:05,008
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you actually start thinking about it, in a measured manner, and stop ignoring it, it will cease to scare you.

544
00:34:05,508 --> 00:34:09,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize, well, whether I'm afraid or not, this is the... like a factual state of affairs.

545
