561
00:34:52,538 --> 00:35:01,021
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I said you are responsible for that, because you keep yourself pretty much intoxicated, like a drunk man, with life, with health, and with youth.

562
00:35:04,282 --> 00:35:06,564
[SPEAKER_00]: You know it will go away, but you don't think about it.

563
00:35:06,704 --> 00:35:09,606
[SPEAKER_00]: And by not thinking about it, you intoxicate yourself with it while it's present.

564
00:35:10,466 --> 00:35:12,668
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why a healthy man always thinks he'll be healthy.

565
00:35:13,648 --> 00:35:18,472
[SPEAKER_00]: And even when you think about non-health, you think like in a second-person manner, not like something that directly applies to me.

566
00:35:18,872 --> 00:35:23,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I start thinking, no, no, no, it's this health, it's this youth, you're gonna feel that.

567
00:35:25,136 --> 00:35:32,001
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, you're so used to avoiding what you feel, or only chasing what you want to feel, that by default you will be turning away from it.

568
00:35:32,950 --> 00:35:38,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the way to compare the practice of dhamma is going against the grain, you know, against the stream.

569
00:35:39,552 --> 00:35:43,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like denying everything, but basically seeing where things are rooted and then stop ignoring it.

570
00:35:44,014 --> 00:35:46,515
[SPEAKER_00]: By not ignoring it, you see the origin of this.

571
00:35:47,235 --> 00:35:50,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm intoxicated because now I'm healthy, but do I have even say in this health?

572
00:35:50,836 --> 00:35:51,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Who knows?

573
00:35:51,176 --> 00:35:53,337
[SPEAKER_00]: I might be having cancer growing in me or something like that.

574
00:35:54,337 --> 00:35:54,998
[SPEAKER_00]: You get scared.

575
00:35:55,018 --> 00:35:57,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the only reason you got scared is because you never... you refuse to think about it.

576
00:35:59,339 --> 00:36:01,620
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the most certain thing, by the way, when you think about it.

577
00:36:02,413 --> 00:36:02,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Death.

578
00:36:03,894 --> 00:36:05,976
[SPEAKER_00]: As soon as you're born, it's absolutely a must.

579
00:36:06,737 --> 00:36:07,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else is optional.

580
00:36:08,538 --> 00:36:11,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the most certain thing is like the last thing on people's minds.

581
00:36:13,822 --> 00:36:20,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they choose to put it last, and keep it last, and press it there to be last, because it's too unpleasant to admit it.

582
00:36:21,468 --> 00:36:24,551
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you train yourself towards that unpleasantness, you won't be unpleasant anymore.

583
00:36:24,951 --> 00:36:26,012
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only unpleasant because you...

584
00:36:27,133 --> 00:36:29,455
[SPEAKER_00]: The dog chases you only because you start running away from it.

585
00:36:30,236 --> 00:36:32,017
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stand your ground, he won't chase you.

586
00:36:32,876 --> 00:36:33,737
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't even attack you.

587
00:36:34,617 --> 00:36:36,498
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you give in to the run, he will go after you.

588
00:36:37,079 --> 00:36:40,621
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly... You start giving in to avoiding this, and that's how he goes after you.

589
00:36:43,343 --> 00:36:45,864
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it, too.

590
00:36:45,884 --> 00:36:47,265
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I know.

591
00:36:47,325 --> 00:36:48,066
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just very interesting.

592
00:36:48,086 --> 00:36:48,466
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, yeah.

593
00:36:48,486 --> 00:36:49,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

594
00:36:49,847 --> 00:36:50,347
[SPEAKER_00]: You're welcome.

595
00:36:50,507 --> 00:36:51,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Good questions.

